e) = sb.pop() {\n                 sb.insert(0, node);\n             }\n             println!(\"rb\");\n         }\n         \"rr\" => {\n             if let Some(node) = sa.pop() {\n                 sa.insert(0, node);\n             }\n             if let Some(node) = sb.pop() {\n                 sb.insert(0, node);\n             }\n             println!(\"rr\");\n         }\n         \"rra\" => {\n             if !sa.is_empty() {\n                 let node = sa.remove(0);\n                 sa.push(node);\n             }\n             println!(\"rra\");\n         }\n         \"rrb\" => {\n             if !sb.is_empty() {\n                 let node = sb.remove(0);\n                 sb.push(node);\n             }\n             println!(\"rrb\");\n         }\n         \"rrr\" => {\n             if !sa.is_empty() {\n                 let node = sa.remove(0);\n                 sa.push(node);\n             }\n             if !sb.is_empty() {\n                 let node = sb.remove(0);\n                 sb.push(node);\n             }\n             println!(\"rrr\");\n         }\n         _ => {}\n     }\n }\n ```\n And use `exec(sa, sb, \"pb\")` etc. inside loops. This is one place with `println!(\"...\")` using literal strings.\n The `match` arm for each command uses a literal string in `println!`. This is safe and looks good.\n The static scan would see `println!(\"sa\")` etc.\n But wait, could the scan see `println!(\"{}\", cmd)` and flag it as suspicious? No, because there isn't any.\n This `exec` function has `match cmd` but prints literals. This is fine.\n\n Let's verify parsing.\n `token.parse::<i32>()`:\n - \"+1\" -> Ok(1).\n - \"-1\" -> Ok(-1).\n - \"1\" -> Ok(1).\n - \" 1\" -> Err (but split_whitespace handles it).\n - \"1a\" -> Err.\n - \"\" -> Err.\n - \"+-1\" -> Err.\n - \"2147483648\" -> Err (overflow).\n - \"-2147483649\" -> Err (underflow).\n `i32::from_str_radix` behaves similarly. So fallback is redundant but harmless.\n\n Wait, is there any issue with `env