of b1=1 subset: 2, 6.\n       Final bottom-to-top order after ra's: 2, 6.\n       So it IS preserved for that subset.\n       Wait, in [7,3,1,5,2,6], 2 is above 6? No, 6 is top (index 5). 2 is at index 4. So from bottom to top, we encounter 2 before 6.\n       The subset is [2, 6] in bottom-to-top order.\n       After ra's, we get A=[2,6,...]. So bottom-to-top is [2,6]. Preserved.\n       The distinction: when we process the top 2 elements of a stack by `ra`, what happens to their relative order bottom-to-top?\n       Original: [x, y] where x is below y, y is top. (Wait, if there are only 2, y is top, x is bottom).\n       ra(y): [y, x]. Now y is bottom, x is top.\n       ra(x): [x, y]. Back to original.\n       So processing a suffix of the stack preserves the order of those elements at the bottom.\n       In general, processing the top k elements of a stack of size n by `ra` if they all match: they end up at the bottom in the same relative order? Let's check with 3.\n       A=[a,b,c] (a=bo